inline.s:
To leave the home life is easy, to keep the rules hard;
with faith and vow all absent, all is marred.
To hold no pure precepts is labor in vain;
though the head be shaved, the toil brings no gain.

To cultivate is easy, to meet a teacher hard;
if no clear teacher comes, all is marred.
Clever in your own conceit, you waste your strength;
blind and sightless your practice brings no gain.

To take on dust is easy, to leave the dust hard;
if toils of dust are unbroken, all is marred.
To court conditions with feeling and habit is strength in vain;
no fruit of the Way is won, the toil brings no gain.

To hear is easy, to make the heart true hard;
to slight one's honored teacher, all is marred.
Proud and self-exalted, you waste your strength;
though your wit cover the world, it brings no gain.

To study the Way is easy, to awaken to it hard;
if no effort is applied, all is marred.
To believe but not to practice wastes your strength;
empty talk and argument bring no gain.

Reciting the Buddha's name is easy, a true heart hard;
when mouth and mind disagree, all is marred.
The mouth recites Amitābha, the mind wanders astray;
though the throat be shouted hoarse, the merit slips away.

To bow to the Buddha is easy, a reverent heart hard;
if the mind is not devout, all is marred.
Five limbs hung in emptiness waste your strength;
though the skull be smashed, it brings no gain.

To recite the sutras is easy, to understand them hard;
to chant without understanding leaves all marred.
To understand but not to practice is strength in vain;
though ten thousand scrolls a day be chanted, the toil brings no gain.
